**Example 10.** Let \(X\) and \(Y\) have a circular normal distribution with \(\sigma=1000\). Find the probability that the point \((X,Y)\) falls within a circle having a radius equal to \(540\) whose center is displaced \(1210\) from the mean of the circular normal distribution.

In units of \(\sigma\), the radius and displacement from the center are, respectively, \(R=\frac{540}{1000}=.54\) and \(r=\frac{1210}{1000}=1.21\). The problem is thus reduced to finding the probability of \(X\) and \(Y\) falling in a circle of radius \(R=.54\) displaced \(r=1.21\) from the center of the distribution where \(\sigma=1\)